WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.719
Film Courage: Scripts are just awful when
the writer tells you everything?

00:00:02.719 --> 00:00:06.010
Wendy Kram, Owner of LA4Hire: Did I say that?

00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:16.189
Oh…a friend of mine said that who she’s
not a screenwriter, she’s not a producer,

00:00:16.189 --> 00:00:18.289
she’s been in the business.

00:00:18.289 --> 00:00:23.810
She’s a publicist and she has exquisite
taste in films.

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:29.519
And I remember asking her because I was asked
for I think a topic of an article and I said

00:00:29.519 --> 00:00:35.380
“What would you say is one of the things
that has made you want to get involved with

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:36.590
a script.

00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:40.430
And what do you think makes a script really
good?”

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:46.440
And she said “What makes scripts awful is
when characters tell you everything.”

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:59.120
And so if a husband and a wife and let’s
say a wife saw her husband having lunch with

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:02.960
another woman that she didn’t know about.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:05.320
And he comes home from work and he’s like
“Hi, Honey?

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:07.190
How was your day?”

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:13.460
And if she were to say…what she will probably
say in real life was “Nothing.”

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:21.180
With that kind of…and Mad Men is the master
of subtext and I recommend looking at just

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:23.920
about any episode of Mad Men.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:32.100
Much less interesting…Or the husband comes
in and says “What did you do today?”

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:37.850
And I was in a similar situation once and
I did not say “I saw you having lunch with

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:40.070
another woman today.”

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:48.440
I wanted him…I wanted to maybe catch him
in a lie.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:51.900
I said “What did you do today?”

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:53.690
And he said “Nothing special.”

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:56.310
“Did you have lunch somewhere interesting?”

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:59.420
“What are you talking about?”

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:04.740
And like maybe one of the things that I love
is that if you really play along that you

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:11.250
can also play with the audience in terms of
thinking “Oh yeah, he was having an affair.

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:21.490
It was an interior decorator and he was surprising
his wife with he just bought a little vacation

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:23.190
home.

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:28.230
And he was making sure or he was renting in
Malibu a place and he wanted to make sure

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:31.140
all the stuff was special for their anniversary.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:37.220
And then what can happen is she keeps testing
him and testing him and starts bringing up

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:44.620
old stuff about how he pisses her off, that
scene could end with not telling her what

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:47.780
the surprise was and the couple deciding “You
never trust me.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:52.910
You know what, I was going to do something
nice for you and scr*w you!

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:54.680
I want a divorce.”

00:02:54.680 --> 00:03:02.849
I’m just spinning here and I’m not saying
that that’s the greatest scene but that’s

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:09.150
an example when you play with subtext versus
characters saying everything that they’re

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:10.150
thinking.

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:16.120
Another example if you take A STREET CAR NAMED
DESIRE with Blanch and Stanley and Stella

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:24.130
and if you had Blanch saying “Well, I really
have been working and I’m

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:27.930
depressed and I have no where else to live.”

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:28.930
Game over!

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:36.090
So if you think about it in those terms.

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:40.590
And it’s really reflecting how people are
in real life, people don’t always say exactly

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:41.739
what they are thinking and feeling.

00:03:41.739 --> 00:03:43.440
Film Courage: Oh, not at all.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:45.700
But that’s what makes it so interesting,
too.

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:52.520
Because you said going along on the ride wondering
will there be a raised eyebrow that will reveal

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:53.520
something?

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:59.650
Wendy Kram: Exactly because then it just has
this flat tone and it really doesn’t replicate

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:03.709
human life and I feel like that’s one of
the things that said earlier when you asked

00:04:03.709 --> 00:04:09.490
what I love about movies is that we can vicariously
put ourselves in the other character’s situation.

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:14.730
And we can say “I’ve been in a situation
like that.

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:20.489
I didn’t want to confront my husband with
another woman” and so on and so forth.

00:04:20.489 --> 00:04:26.430
Film Courage: Or like you can tell if there
is gossip or somebody said something about

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:28.559
you, so they come in “How are you?”

00:04:28.559 --> 00:04:30.020
Wendy Kram: Oh, exactly!

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:33.080
Film Courage: And you know that they want
something from that statement.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:42.050
Wendy Kram: And then we can immerse ourselves
in the person who is suspecting that maybe

00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:45.080
they were talking about her but they are not
coming out and saying it.

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:53.599
But I think that’s such as great point that
it really engages us so much more and because

00:04:53.599 --> 00:04:56.360
that’s a comment that came up before.

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:57.560
You want to be engaged.

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:03.599
I mean, good writing, good script, scripts
that command attention with an extraordinary

00:05:03.599 --> 00:05:11.610
capacity to engage your reader and that’s
usually through really smart dialogue like

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:12.930
what you were talking about.

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:20.159
Film Courage: You talked about too scripts
that don’t propel the character forward.

00:05:20.159 --> 00:05:26.590
So can we have some examples of ones that
do it and ones that don’t or just some themes?

00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:39.819
Wendy Kram: So gosh, okay…Breaking Bad is
such a great example of an escalation of situations.

00:05:39.819 --> 00:05:44.610
So I would say that in the character, every
step that he takes there’s a consequence

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:49.469
and it leads to another and another and then
the character and then the character might

00:05:49.469 --> 00:05:57.379
try to get himself out of it but it’s kind
of like the notion of McBeth.

00:05:57.379 --> 00:06:05.940
Once you create or do something bad, it has
its own momentum.

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:12.189
It’s hard to say without really doing specifics
but basically every scene you want to feel

00:06:12.189 --> 00:06:17.289
that you’re advancing the story, you’re
advancing the plot.

00:06:17.289 --> 00:06:26.550
Even if the character has a set back that
there’s still…one of the best ways to

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:33.610
think about it is that there’s the core
spine of the story where the character, where

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:41.370
you may have an ensemble that each has a goal
that there is a primary objective that the

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:48.529
character is trying to obtain and the obstacles
that get in the way that prevent them from

00:06:48.529 --> 00:06:49.529
that.

00:06:49.529 --> 00:06:53.729
And how they (even if it’s a step back)
but then they’re still trying to overcome

00:06:53.729 --> 00:06:57.449
that obstacle.

00:06:57.449 --> 00:07:04.429
Most movies (successful movies) follow that
formula.

00:07:04.429 --> 00:07:09.740
I hate to say formula because I don’t like
things being formulaic.

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:16.150
And an example really where stories are not
propelled forward is when the writer loses

00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:18.529
focus (the focused is too diffused).

00:07:18.529 --> 00:07:25.319
And one of the common reasons for that would
be when there are too many characters.

00:07:25.319 --> 00:07:32.379
When a writer is introducing so many characters
that rather than get to know one or a few

00:07:32.379 --> 00:07:36.419
really well, we’re getting glimpses of all
of these different characters but none of

00:07:36.419 --> 00:07:41.259
them (because they don’t have time to develop
them particularly well) and so we don’t

00:07:41.259 --> 00:07:45.979
have a core sense of a character’s primary
focus or a group of character’s primary

00:07:45.979 --> 00:07:46.979
focus.

00:07:46.979 --> 00:07:50.490
And the goal is what are the obstacles that
get in their way.

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:57.689
So I think any time there’s a really diffuse
focus, then scripts don’t…they wind up

00:07:57.689 --> 00:08:03.229
just being very dispersed, rather than having
a through line.

